WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:10.110
There's a roughly $91 billion
of taxable income underreported

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:11.010
each year.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.780
Our goal is to look
at that type of thing

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.560
by looking at how tax
evasion schemes change,

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.570
in relation to IRS regulations.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:20.370
And through this
process, our goal

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:24.030
is to anticipate new iterations
of abusive tax shelters or tax

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:25.740
evasion schemes, what have you.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.360
The government
knows that as soon

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.860
as they change the
regulations, they

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:31.530
know there's going to come
up a new tax evasion scheme.

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:34.620
So for them the question
is, can we in some way

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:36.990
be one step ahead,
instead of one step

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:39.900
behind, when it comes
to finding tax evaders?

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.430
Our system is
computer-aided law.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:44.040
And in the past, it's been
looking at past behavior

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:46.339
in trying to look for
similar past behavior.

00:00:46.339 --> 00:00:47.880
What they're not
doing is they're not

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.660
looking for possible modes
of exploitation that exist

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.352
that haven't been seen yet.

00:00:53.352 --> 00:00:54.810
So what we're doing,
is essentially

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:57.374
acting as a tax
evader that is working

00:00:57.374 --> 00:00:59.790
on the government's side,
almost like an undercover agent.

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:01.680
We're saying, if
we were somebody

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.560
that had a very strong
incentive to evade tax,

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.470
or to iterate through the
current system of laws

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:10.590
in order to generate tax
savings, what would we do?

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:14.250
Computer science can offer
a lot to law, in a sense,

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:18.160
because you can see the
law says, if you do this,

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:20.190
then this happens,
else this happens.

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:23.580
So it's quite amenable, all
these human regulations,

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.960
to try to write them
in as source code.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:28.940
We're trying to
represent what's written

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:31.710
in the tax law in the software.

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.420
One way to describe
it bluntly is

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.400
to say we have a
calculator for tax.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.460
We use this calculator to do
simulations, as in saying,

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:40.440
we put in different
inputs of a transaction

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.510
and then see what
is our tax output.

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:44.160
Then we can see if
we can optimize it.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:45.576
So that's where
we're saying, this

00:01:45.576 --> 00:01:49.080
is how we find these
transaction sequences that will

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.060
pay the least amount of tax.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.610
It's not that far-fetched to
think, as some people say,

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:54.980
the law is code.

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.730
And then trying to
figure out how can you,

00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:58.230
from a computer
science perspective,

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:01.230
improve this essential
document of civilization as you

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:04.580
can describe the law to be.

